  "estimatedMinutes": 1,
  "statement": "What is the value of:\n\n\\[ \\dfrac{1}{3} \\cdot \\dfrac{2}{4} \\cdot \\dfrac{3}{5} \\cdots \\dfrac{18}{20} \\cdot \\dfrac{19}{21} \\cdot \\dfrac{20}{22} \\]",
  "answerChoices": [
    {
      "label": "A",
      "text": "\\(\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{462}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "B",
      "text": "\\(\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{231}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "C",
      "text": "\\(\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{132}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "D",
      "text": "\\(\\displaystyle \\dfrac{2}{213}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "E",
      "text": "\\(\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{22}\\)"
    }
  ],
  "answer": {
    "letter": "b",
    "value": "1/231"
  },
  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.amc-8-2022-p8.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "Since every integer from \\(3\\) to \\(20\\) occurs once as a denominator and once as a numerator, they cancel each other out.\n\nAfter canceling every number out, we have only \\(1\\) and \\(2\\) left as numerators and \\(21\\) and \\(22\\) left as denominators.\n\nThe remaining fraction is \\( \\dfrac{1 \\cdot 2}{21 \\cdot 22} .\\) This simplifies to \\( \\dfrac{2}{462} = \\dfrac{1}{231} \\)\n\nThus, the correct answer is B.",
      "hints": [
        "Most numerator and denominator factors cancel",
        "After cancellation, only \\(1\\cdot2\\) remains on top"
      ],
      "images": []
    }
  ],
